Windows Utility – Release Path1. Product PositioningTarget both mainstream Windows users and technical power users using a dual-surface distribution model.    • Mainstream Layer:• Clean landing page• Simple installer (.exe)• Clear screenshots + short demo video• One-click download button    • Technical Layer:• GitHub repository for transparency• Changelog + version history• Issue tracker• Source visibility (optional)2. Distribution Infrastructure    • Hosting:• GitHub Releases for installer hosting• Simple static website (domain landing page)• Optional CDN / object storage (S3, R2, etc.)    • Files Required:• latest.json (version metadata)• Windows_Utility_X.X.X.exe (installer)3. Auto-Update Flow    • Application Logic:• On startup ? GET latest.json• Compare semantic version• If newer ? prompt user• Download installer with progress bar• Launch installer and exit4. Launch Sequence    • Phase 1 – Pre-Release:• Stabilise core features• Remove debug prints• Add versioning system    • Phase 2 – Soft Launch:• Publish GitHub repo• Upload first release• Share demo on Reddit / niche communities    • Phase 3 – Public Launch:• SEO-focused landing page• Short demo video• Submit to software directories (MajorGeeks, etc.)5. Strategic BoundariesKeep architecture primarily local-first. Avoid premature SaaS complexity. Auto-update is justified. Full account cloud sync is not required at current scope.6. Long-Term Optional Expansions    • • Optional export/import settings bundle (.wup)    • • Optional licensing layer (if monetised)    • • Crash reporting endpointFor a Windows utility like yours, forced accounts create friction with little perceived value.Typical user reaction to “Sign in required” for a desktop tool:    • Suspicion (“Why does this need my email?”)    • Privacy concern    • Drop-off    • Immediate uninstallEspecially for:    • System utilities    • Low-level tools    • Explorer / desktop modifiersThose categories are expected to be offline and lightweight.Where Accounts Make SenseAccounts are justified when:    • Data must live in the cloud    • Cross-device continuity is core value    • Collaboration is required    • Paid licensing needs enforcementYour utility is:    • Local-state heavy    • Machine-specific    • Resolution / hardware dependent    • OS-context dependentSo login doesn’t unlock meaningful value.Psychological Advantage of No AccountIf your landing page says:No account. No tracking. Works offline.That increases trust.Especially in the Windows utility ecosystem.Power users actively prefer:    • Portable apps    • No installers (optional)    • No telemetry    • No login wallsClean Product IdentityYour current best positioning:Local-first. No accounts. No cloud dependency.Optional auto-update for convenience.That reads as:    • Respectful    • Professional    • Efficient    • Not extractiveWhich fits what you're building.Auto-update = maintenance layer.Accounts = business model layer.You don’t need the second to ship a strong product.